# Shapes: scale_shape

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_shape()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_shape.html), which covers
`scale_shape_binned()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code
first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what
it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_shape()` maps a discrete column to six shapes that are easy to tell apart, solid by default
or hollow; a seventh value and any after it are not drawn, with a warning, and
`scale_shape_manual()` gives shapes by hand. A continuous column can be mapped to shapes only
through `scale_shape_binned()`, which cuts it into bins first, and since shapes have no order even
that is not advised. A shape can be named by number or by name, and only shapes 21 to 25 take a
fill. Giggly has `Giggly.Plot.scale_shape_discrete/2`, with its `solid:`, and
`Giggly.Plot.scale_shape_manual/2`, a shape named by number or by name as an atom, but not ggplot2's
short name, `scale_shape()`, nor binned or identity shape scales (`scale_shape_binned()`,
`scale_shape_identity()`), and by default maps `shape` to a discrete column as ggplot2's default
scale does, refusing a seventh value, and draws the legend that reads it back.

## A shape for each cut

The examples draw a random sample of the `diamonds` dataset, picked after `set.seed(596)`.

```r
set.seed(596)
dsmall <- diamonds[sample(nrow(diamonds), 100), ]

(d <- ggplot(dsmall, aes(carat, price)) + geom_point(aes(shape = cut)))
d + scale_shape(solid = TRUE) # the default
d + scale_shape(solid = FALSE)
d + scale_shape(name = "Cut of diamond")
```

`name` titles the legend. `scale_shape()` is `Giggly.Plot.scale_shape_discrete/2`, which takes `solid:` and `name:`. Not in
Giggly yet: choosing the order of a column's values (`cut` is an ordered factor).

## The order of the shapes

```r
# To change order of levels, change order of
# underlying factor
levels(dsmall$cut) <- c("Fair", "Good", "Very Good", "Premium", "Ideal")

# Need to recreate plot to pick up new data
ggplot(dsmall, aes(price, carat)) + geom_point(aes(shape = cut))
```

Shapes go to a column's values in the order of its levels.

Not in Giggly yet: choosing the order of a column's values.

## The available shapes

```r
# Show a list of available shapes
df_shapes <- data.frame(shape = 0:24)
ggplot(df_shapes, aes(0, 0, shape = shape)) +
  geom_point(aes(shape = shape), size = 5, fill = 'red') +
  scale_shape_identity() +
  facet_wrap(~shape) +
  theme_void()
```

`scale_shape_identity()` draws each point in the shape its number names, and `facet_wrap()`
gives each a panel of its own. Giggly has `facet_wrap()`: `facet_wrap(~shape)` is
`Giggly.Plot.facet_wrap(plot, :shape)`.

Not in Giggly yet: `scale_shape_identity()` and `theme_void()`.